## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  GUI.INC.BL -- three dialogs, in a box that puts the screen back.
## **
## **      GUI.YN      a question, and a Y or N answer
## **      GUI.MENU    a question, and a row chosen from a list
## **      GUI.TEXT    a question, and a line of typed text
## **
## **  These are the three things a program interrupts itself to ask, and
## **  each is otherwise a dozen lines of geometry at every call site. The
## **  work they share is the BOX: measure it against the text, centre it,
## **  save what it covers, draw a frame, and give the pixels back on the
## **  way out. That is GUI.OPEN and GUI.CLOSE, and the three entry points
## **  are thin.
## **
## **  IT OWNS NO INTERACTION LOOP. GUI.MENU is MENUHELP.RUN inside a
## **  frame, GUI.TEXT is INPHELP.GET inside a frame, and only GUI.YN --
## **  which is four keys -- reads the keyboard itself. A dialog library
## **  that reimplemented a menu would be a second menu to fix.
## **
## **  REQUIRES, and does not #INCLUDE for you, in this order:
## **      GPB.INC.BL        GP.BOX, GP.FILL, GP.PRINTAT, GP.STASH, GP.CALL
## **      THEME.INC.BL      the colour roles, and GOSUB THEME.LOAD first
## **      MENUHELP.INC.BL   GUI.MENU only
## **      INPHELP.INC.BL    GUI.TEXT only
## **  Leave one out and you only pay for what you left in -- but you must
## **  include what you call.
## **
## **  Usage, the whole of it:
## **      THEME.DARK = 0 : GOSUB THEME.LOAD
## **      GUI.BANK = 8
## **      GUI.MSG$ = "Delete the file?"
## **      GOSUB GUI.YN
## **      IF GUI.ANSWER = 1 THEN <yes>
## **
## **   in   GUI.MSG$        the question. "" for none
## **        GUI.MSG2$       a second line, for the detail that does not
## **                        fit. "" for none, and then there is no gap
## **        GUI.TITLE$      a name in the top edge of the frame. "" for
## **                        none
## **        GUI.BANK        a spare RAM bank to save the covered cells in.
## **                        0 MEANS DO NOT SAVE, and then the box is still
## **                        on the screen when the call returns -- see the
## **                        note on that below
## **        GUI.STYLE       0, the default, draws the single line box.
## **                        1 to 5 are GP.BOX's other styles. GP.BOX's own
## **                        style 0 -- the solid block -- is not offered
## **                        here; a dialog wants an edge, not a slab
## **        GUI.PANEL.IN    the panel: the box's own background, and the
## **                        colour the message lines are written in. 0
## **                        takes THEME.TEXT
## **        GUI.BORDER.IN   the frame's attribute. 0 takes THEME.BORDER
## **        GUI.GLYPH       0 frames the box with GP.BOX, which is right on
## **                        the stock charset. Non-zero frames it from six
## **                        glyphs of the CALLER'S, and then GUI.STYLE is
## **                        not consulted:
## **                            GUI.EDGE.H      the horizontal run
## **                            GUI.EDGE.V      the vertical run
## **                            GUI.CORNER.TL   top left
## **                            GUI.CORNER.TR   top right
## **                            GUI.CORNER.BL   bottom left
## **                            GUI.CORNER.BR   bottom right
## **                        Those are GP.FILL ARGUMENTS, not tile numbers.
## **                        GP.FILL converts PETSCII to a screen code, so a
## **                        glyph parked at tile $C0 is asked for as $80 --
## **                        the convention samples/editor already uses
## **        GUI.PLACE       0 centres the box. Non-zero uses GUI.X, GUI.Y
## **        GUI.X  GUI.Y    top left, when GUI.PLACE says so
## **        GUI.ROW.OFFSET      added to the row the box lands on, however it
## **                        was placed. 0 for almost everybody -- see the
## **                        note on scrolled maps below
## **
## **   out  GUI.KEY         the key that ended it, whichever call
## **        GUI.LEFT GUI.TOP   where the box actually went
## **        GUI.WIDTH  GUI.HEIGHT    and how big it turned out
## **
## **  GUI.ROW.OFFSET IS FOR A CALLER THAT SCROLLS THE MAP IN HARDWARE. The GP
## **  drawing commands address the TEXT MAP, and VERA's L1_VSCROLL decides
## **  which map row is shown as screen row 0. A program using that to
## **  scroll -- samples/editor moves a whole document by writing three
## **  rows -- has a map row that is not its screen row, and a dialog
## **  centred against the screen would be drawn wherever the map happens
## **  to be sitting. Such a caller sets GUI.ROW.OFFSET to VSCROLL/8 and gets
## **  centred against what the user can actually see. Everyone else leaves
## **  it 0 and never learns this paragraph exists.
## **
## **  THE PANEL IS ONE ATTRIBUTE, not a background from one theme role and
## **  text from another. It used to fill with THEME.PAGE and write with
## **  THEME.TEXT, which is invisible for as long as those two share a
## **  background -- and in the stock palettes they do. samples/editor is
## **  where that stopped being true: it re-points the roles at its own
## **  chrome, PAGE at a light grey it does not even use (its own comment
## **  says so) and TEXT at the document's white-on-black. The dialog came
## **  up as a grey slab in the middle of a black editor. So the fill and
## **  the text are now the same attribute, and a program whose roles mean
## **  something else can say which one.
## **
## **  .IN IS "WHAT THE CALLER ASKED FOR". GUI.PANEL.IN and GUI.BORDER.IN
## **  are the requests; GUI.PANEL and GUI.BORDER are what GUI.OPEN settled
## **  on, and those two are what everything downstream draws with --
## **  including GUI.FRAME, and including a caller borrowing GUI.FRAME on
## **  its own. Both requests are 0-MEANS-DEFAULT: attribute 0 is black on
## **  black, legal and no use to anybody, so it is free to spend as "I did
## **  not choose". A caller that never heard of either gets the theme.
## **
## **  CENTRED AGAINST THE MODE, NOT AGAINST 80x60. The size comes from
## **  screen_mode with carry set, asked at every call, so a program that
## **  changes mode half way through does not start placing dialogs off the
## **  edge. It costs one KERNAL call on a path that is already waiting for
## **  a human.
## **
## **  THE BOX SAVES 2 BYTES A CELL INTO ONE 8K BANK, so at most 4094 cells
## **  fit -- 63x63, far larger than any dialog. A box bigger than that is
## **  NOT an error and NOT a corrupted bank: the save is skipped, the
## **  dialog still runs, and GUI.STASHED comes back 0 so the caller can
## **  tell it must repaint. The same is true when GUI.BANK is 0.
## **
## **  UPPER CASE, ON THE DEFAULT CHARSET. GP.PRINTAT converts PETSCII to
## **  a screen code, and BASLOAD writes string literals through as the
## **  ASCII bytes that were in the source -- so on charset 2, the one the
## **  machine boots with, ASCII lower case lands on the graphics half of
## **  the font and comes out as line fragments. Measured: "Confirm" wrote
## **  tiles 3, 79, 78, 70, 73, 82, 77, of which only the C was a letter.
## **  The default answers above are upper case for that reason.
## **
## **  MIXED CASE COSTS THE FRAME. Turning ISO mode on (bit 6 of $0372)
## **  makes GP.PRINTAT pass bytes through untouched, so lower case works
## **  -- but the ISO charset is ASCII-ordered, and GP.BOX draws from
## **  screen codes $40-$7D, which in that order are letters. The corner
## **  tile is 112 either way; in ISO that glyph is a "p". So: upper case
## **  with a frame, or mixed case without one -- unless the caller
## **  re-orders the font, which is what samples/editor does. Then GP.BOX's
## **  table points at the wrong glyphs, and GUI.GLYPH is how the caller
## **  says where the right ones went. That is the whole reason the frame
## **  is overridable.
## **
## **  The whole GUI.* name space belongs to this file.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF GUI.DEFS
#DEFINE GUI.DEFS 1

## screen_mode. With carry SET it REPORTS rather than sets: mode in A,
## columns in X, rows in Y. The same entry point APPHELP uses, named here
## so GUI does not depend on APPHELP being included.
#DEFINE GUI.SCRMODE $FF5F

## The frame GUI.STYLE 0 means. GP.BOX style 2 is the single line.
#DEFINE GUI.LINEBOX 2

## A bank is 8K and a cell is 2 bytes, so this is the ceiling on a saved
## rectangle. See the note in the interface block.
#DEFINE GUI.MAXCELLS 4094

## Air INSIDE the frame: columns each side, rows above and below. Text
## packed against a border is hard to read, and a dialog that is bigger
## than its contents reads as deliberate rather than as a tight fit. One
## place to change, and every box changes with it.
#DEFINE GUI.PADX 2
#DEFINE GUI.PADY 1

## Keys GUI.YN answers to. Y and N without case, and both ways of saying
## "no thanks" -- ESC and STOP -- which answer NO rather than hanging.
#DEFINE GUI.UY 89
#DEFINE GUI.LY 121
#DEFINE GUI.UN 78
#DEFINE GUI.LN 110

## ...and the other pair, for GUI.OKCANCEL. O and C.
#DEFINE GUI.UO 79
#DEFINE GUI.LO 111
#DEFINE GUI.UC 67
#DEFINE GUI.LC 99
#DEFINE GUI.ESCAPE 27
#DEFINE GUI.STOP 3
#DEFINE GUI.RETURN 13
#DEFINE GUI.SPACE 32

GOTO GUI.MODULE.END

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.YN -- a question, and a Y or N answer.
## **
## **   in   GUI.MSG$ GUI.MSG2$ GUI.TITLE$ and the box inputs
## **        GUI.HINT$   the answers, under the question. "" takes the
## **                    default, "YES      NO". Whatever it says, the
## **                    first Y and the first N in it are lit as the keys
## **
## **        GUI.OKCANCEL  0 asks YES/NO on the Y and N keys, which is the
## **                      default. Non-zero asks OK/CANCEL on O and C,
## **                      and then RETURN also accepts -- see the note
## **                      by that test for why only then
## **
## **   out  GUI.ANSWER  1 for yes or OK, 0 for no or cancel
## **        GUI.KEY     the key itself, so a caller that cares whether
## **                    the no was ESC or N can still tell
## **
## **  ESC AND STOP ANSWER NO. A dialog that can only be answered is a
## **  dialog that can trap someone, and "no" is the safe reading of
## **  walking away from a question.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.YN:

## ---- which pair of answers, and therefore which two keys. Everything
## ---- below works off GUI.YES/GUI.NO, so the two modes differ in four
## ---- assignments and one default string rather than in two routines.
    GUI.YES = GUI.UY : GUI.YES.ALT = GUI.LY
    GUI.NO = GUI.UN : GUI.NO.ALT = GUI.LN
    GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$ = "YES      NO"
    IF GUI.OKCANCEL = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.YN.SET
    GUI.YES = GUI.UO : GUI.YES.ALT = GUI.LO
    GUI.NO = GUI.UC : GUI.NO.ALT = GUI.LC
    GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$ = "OK      CANCEL"

GUI.YN.SET:
    GUI.HINT.LINE$ = GUI.HINT$
    IF GUI.HINT.LINE$ = "" THEN GUI.HINT.LINE$ = GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = 1 : GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN
    GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)) / 2)
    IF GUI.HINT.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.HINT.LINE$, THEME.CLR(THEME.DIMMED)
    GUI.MARK.CH = GUI.YES : GOSUB GUI.YN.MARK
    GUI.MARK.CH = GUI.NO : GOSUB GUI.YN.MARK
    GUI.ANSWER = 0

GUI.YN.WAIT:
    GET GUI.PRESSED$
    IF GUI.PRESSED$ = "" THEN GOTO GUI.YN.WAIT
    GUI.KEY = ASC(GUI.PRESSED$)
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.YES THEN GUI.ANSWER = 1 : GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.YES.ALT THEN GUI.ANSWER = 1 : GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.NO THEN GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.NO.ALT THEN GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.ESCAPE THEN GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.STOP THEN GOTO GUI.YN.DONE

## ---- RETURN accepts, but ONLY in OK/CANCEL. A dialog offering OK has a
## ---- default and RETURN is how everyone takes it. A YES/NO question is
## ---- being asked precisely because there is no default, so RETURN there
## ---- would answer for the user -- and the question is usually the
## ---- destructive one.
    IF GUI.OKCANCEL = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.YN.WAIT
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.RETURN THEN GUI.ANSWER = 1 : GOTO GUI.YN.DONE
    GOTO GUI.YN.WAIT

GUI.YN.DONE:
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.YN.MARK -- repaint one letter of the hint in the key colour.
## **
## **   in   GUI.MARK.CH    the character code to look for
## **        GUI.HINT.LINE$ GUI.HINT.LEFT GUI.INNER.TOP   the line as it was just printed
## **
## **  THE WORD IS THE ANSWER AND THE LETTER IS THE KEY. The line reads
## **  "YES      NO" and the Y and the N are lit, rather than spelling out
## **  "Y = YES" -- which says the same thing twice and makes the reader
## **  parse a legend before answering a question.
## **
## **  FOUND BY SEARCH, not by a fixed column, because GUI.HINT$ lets the
## **  caller write the line -- "YES / NO", "OUI  NON" and "Y/N" all mark
## **  correctly. The FIRST match wins, which is why the answers are in
## **  this order: a "NO" before a "YES" would still light its own N and
## **  Y, but a word containing an earlier Y or N would take the mark.
## **
## **  A line with no Y or no N is not an error, it is simply not marked.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.YN.MARK:
    FOR GUI.INDEX = 1 TO LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
        IF ASC(MID$(GUI.HINT.LINE$, GUI.INDEX, 1)) = GUI.MARK.CH THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT + GUI.INDEX - 1, GUI.INNER.TOP, MID$(GUI.HINT.LINE$, GUI.INDEX, 1), THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE) : RETURN
    NEXT GUI.INDEX
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.MENU -- a question, and a row chosen from a list.
## **
## **   in   GUI.COUNT          how many rows
## **        MENUHELP.ITEM$()    the rows, 1..GUI.COUNT
## **        GUI.SEL             the row to start on, 0 for the first
## **        GUI.FLAGS           passed to MENUHELP.FLAGS -- MUSTSEL,
## **                            NOWRAP, GAMEPAD. KEEPMARK is meaningless
## **                            here because the box does not survive
## **
## **   out  GUI.SEL             1..GUI.COUNT, or 0 if cancelled
## **        GUI.KEY             13 chose, 27 cancelled
## **
## **  THE ROWS GO IN MENUHELP.ITEM$ AND THE CALLER DIMS IT, which is
## **  MENUHELP's own contract and is not worth breaking to hide. A second
## **  array here would mean copying every row on every call, and it would
## **  still have to guess a bound -- the exact thing MENUHELP refuses to
## **  do. See the note at the top of MENUHELP.INC.BL.
## **
## **  Items are NOT indented here. MENUHELP prints them at the left edge
## **  of the highlight, so a leading space in the item is what gives the
## **  bar its inset -- that is the caller's choice to make, per row.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.MENU:
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = GUI.COUNT
    GUI.BODY.WIDTH = 0
    FOR GUI.INDEX = 1 TO GUI.COUNT
        IF LEN(MENUHELP.ITEM$(GUI.INDEX)) > GUI.BODY.WIDTH THEN GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(MENUHELP.ITEM$(GUI.INDEX))
    NEXT GUI.INDEX
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN
    MENUHELP.X = GUI.INNER.LEFT : MENUHELP.Y = GUI.INNER.TOP
    MENUHELP.WIDTH = GUI.INNER.WIDTH
    MENUHELP.COUNT = GUI.COUNT
    REM GUI.PANEL -- the panel GUI.OPEN just painted, so the unselected rows are the
    REM same colour as the box they sit in. This was THEME.PAGE once, which in the light
    REM palette is blue on blue: correct for the GP.FILL, where only the background is
    REM ever seen, and invisible for anything with letters in it. Every unselected row
    REM was drawn and unreadable, and only the highlight showed.
    MENUHELP.ATTR = GUI.PANEL
    MENUHELP.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
    MENUHELP.HOTATTR = 0
    MENUHELP.FLAGS = GUI.FLAGS
    MENUHELP.SEL = GUI.SEL
    GOSUB MENUHELP.RUN
    GUI.SEL = MENUHELP.SEL : GUI.KEY = MENUHELP.KEY
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.TEXT -- a question, and a line of typed text.
## **
## **   in   GUI.LEN     how many characters fit. 0 takes 20
## **        GUI.INPUT$  the starting value, edited in place
## **        GUI.MASK    non-zero shows asterisks, for a password
## **
## **   out  GUI.INPUT$  what was typed
## **        GUI.OK      1 if RETURN finished it, 0 if it was cancelled
## **        GUI.KEY     the key itself
## **
## **  THE RESULT IS GUI.INPUT$, NOT GUI.TEXT$, because GUI.TEXT is the
## **  label of this routine and a string of the same name beside it is
## **  the kind of collision that reads fine and compiles into something
## **  else.
## **
## **  The field is drawn in THEME.HILITE, so it is visibly a place to
## **  type rather than more of the message. INPHELP puts the old text
## **  back when ESC cancels, so GUI.INPUT$ is never silently emptied.
## **
## **  THE HINT LINE IS FIXED HERE, unlike GUI.YN's. Its two key names are
## **  painted at known offsets into the line, so a caller-supplied string
## **  would put the highlight on the wrong words.
## **
## **  Cursor up, down and TAB do NOT leave this dialog. They are how
## **  INPHELP moves between the fields of a form; with one field there is
## **  nowhere to go, so they re-enter it. Only RETURN and ESC end it.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.TEXT:
    IF GUI.LEN = 0 THEN GUI.LEN = 20
    GUI.HINT.LINE$ = "ENTER = OK    ESC = CANCEL"
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = 3 : GUI.BODY.WIDTH = GUI.LEN
    IF LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$) > GUI.BODY.WIDTH THEN GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
    GUI.WAS$ = GUI.INPUT$
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN

## ---- the field, centred over the inner width like the hint under it
    GUI.FIELD.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - GUI.LEN) / 2)
    IF GUI.FIELD.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.FIELD.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    INPHELP.X = GUI.FIELD.LEFT : INPHELP.Y = GUI.INNER.TOP
    INPHELP.LEN = GUI.LEN
    INPHELP.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
    INPHELP.TEXT$ = GUI.INPUT$
    INPHELP.MASK = GUI.MASK

## ---- the two keys that end it, named, and the words themselves picked
## ---- out of the dimmed line. Printed at known offsets rather than
## ---- searched for, because unlike GUI.YN's hint this text is fixed --
## ---- see the note above on why it is not overridable.
    GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)) / 2)
    IF GUI.HINT.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, GUI.HINT.LINE$, THEME.CLR(THEME.DIMMED)
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, "ENTER", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT + 14, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, "ESC", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)

## ---- UP, DOWN AND TAB LEAVE AN INPHELP FIELD WITHOUT CANCELLING IT --
## ---- that is how a caller with several fields moves between them, and
## ---- it is right there in INPHELP's interface. With ONE field there is
## ---- nowhere to move to, so they came back here as "not RETURN", which
## ---- read as a cancel: an arrow key silently threw the typing away.
## ---- Go straight back into the same field instead, so the only ways
## ---- out of this dialog are the two the hint line names.
GUI.TEXT.FIELD:
    GOSUB INPHELP.GET
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.DOWN THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.UP THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.TAB THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD

    GUI.INPUT$ = INPHELP.TEXT$
    GUI.KEY = INPHELP.KEY
    GUI.OK = 0
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.RETURN THEN GUI.OK = 1

## ---- A cancel gives back what was passed IN, not what the field held
## ---- when it was last re-entered. INPHELP restores to the value at ITS
## ---- last call, which after an arrow key is not the same thing.
    IF GUI.OK = 0 THEN GUI.INPUT$ = GUI.WAS$
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.OPEN -- measure, place, save, draw, and print the question.
## **
## **   in   GUI.BODY.ROWS   how many rows the caller needs under the message
## **        GUI.BODY.WIDTH  and how wide its widest one is
## **
## **   out  GUI.INNER.LEFT GUI.INNER.TOP   where the caller's own rows start
## **        GUI.INNER.WIDTH          how wide they may be
## **        GUI.STASHED     1 if the covered cells were saved
## **
## **  Public, because a caller wanting a fourth kind of dialog should
## **  build it on this rather than on a copy of it.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.OPEN:
    GOSUB GUI.SIZE
    GOSUB GUI.PLACE.BOX

## ---- Save what the box is about to cover. Both reasons to skip it are
## ---- ordinary, not errors, and both leave GUI.STASHED at 0: no bank was
## ---- offered, or the rectangle is bigger than a bank holds.

    GUI.STASHED = 0
    IF GUI.BANK = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.OPEN.DRAW
    IF GUI.WIDTH * GUI.HEIGHT > GUI.MAXCELLS THEN GOTO GUI.OPEN.DRAW
    GP.STASH GUI.BANK, GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT
    GUI.STASHED = 1

## ---- Fill THEN frame. The fill is what makes the box opaque -- without
## ---- it the frame is a rectangle drawn over whatever was underneath,
## ---- which reads as a mistake rather than as a dialog.

GUI.OPEN.DRAW:
    GUI.BOX.STYLE = GUI.STYLE
    IF GUI.BOX.STYLE = 0 THEN GUI.BOX.STYLE = GUI.LINEBOX
## ---- What the caller asked for, or the theme. Settled ONCE, here, so
## ---- that everything below -- the fill, the frame, the message lines,
## ---- and MENUHELP's unselected rows -- is reading one answer rather
## ---- than each making the same decision again.
    GUI.PANEL = GUI.PANEL.IN
    IF GUI.PANEL = 0 THEN GUI.PANEL = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
    GUI.BORDER = GUI.BORDER.IN
    IF GUI.BORDER = 0 THEN GUI.BORDER = THEME.CLR(THEME.BORDER)
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT, GUI.SPACE, GUI.PANEL
    IF GUI.GLYPH <> 0 THEN GOSUB GUI.FRAME : GOTO GUI.OPEN.TITLE
    GP.BOX GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT, GUI.BOX.STYLE, GUI.BORDER
GUI.OPEN.TITLE:
    IF GUI.TITLE$ <> "" THEN GUI.TITLE.LEFT = GUI.LEFT + INT((GUI.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.TITLE$) - 2) / 2) : GP.PRINTAT GUI.TITLE.LEFT, GUI.TOP, " " + GUI.TITLE$ + " ", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)

## ---- The question, then a blank row before the caller's own rows -- but
## ---- only if there WAS a question. A menu with no message should not
## ---- open with an empty line.

    GUI.INNER.LEFT = GUI.LEFT + 1 + GUI.PADX : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.TOP + 1 + GUI.PADY
    IF GUI.MSG$ <> "" THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.INNER.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.MSG$, GUI.PANEL : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    IF GUI.MSG2$ <> "" THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.INNER.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.MSG2$, GUI.PANEL : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    IF GUI.HEAD.ROWS > 0 THEN GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.FRAME -- the border, drawn from the caller's own six glyphs.
## **
## **  GP.BOX PICKS ITS GLYPHS FROM A TABLE IN THE RUNTIME, so it can only
## **  ever draw the six styles that table holds, at the screen codes the
## **  stock charset puts them at. A program that re-orders the font --
## **  samples/editor does, so that a VERA tile index IS a character code --
## **  moves those glyphs out from under it, and style 2 comes out as
## **  "p @ B". Nothing GP.BOX can be TOLD fixes that, because the table is
## **  not the caller's to change. Naming the six glyphs is.
## **
## **   in   GUI.LEFT GUI.TOP GUI.WIDTH GUI.HEIGHT   the rectangle: left, top, and size
## **        GUI.EDGE.H .. GUI.CORNER.BR            the six glyphs
## **        GUI.BORDER                   the attribute to draw them in
## **
## **   and it works out, from the rectangle:
## **        GUI.RIGHT GUI.BOTTOM              the right column and bottom row,
## **                                     which four of the eight fills and
## **                                     both corners on each edge need
## **
## **  EIGHT FILLS IS THE FLOOR, not a first draft. Six distinct glyphs at
## **  eight places: two runs that repeat one glyph, and four corners that
## **  are one cell each and all different. Nothing merges -- a run drawn
## **  the full width would put a horizontal where a corner goes and need
## **  the corner drawn over it anyway, which is the same eight calls in a
## **  worse order. The only way to draw a frame in ONE call is GP.BOX, and
## **  GP.BOX is the thing this exists because we cannot use.
## **
## **  It is also not eight MORE fills. samples/editor drew exactly these
## **  eight inline for its dropdown; that code is now this call, so the
## **  program has one frame drawer where it used to have two.
## **
## **  GUI.BORDER IS AN INPUT rather than THEME.BORDER read in here, because
## **  the dropdown's frame is the document's own text colour -- a routine
## **  that hard-coded the role could not have been shared with it.
## **
## **  A box under 2x2 has no interior and the runs would be handed a
## **  negative width. Nothing this library builds is that small -- the
## **  padding alone puts the floor at 6x4 -- but the guard is two tests on
## **  a path about to do eight fills.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.FRAME:
    IF GUI.WIDTH < 2 THEN RETURN
    IF GUI.HEIGHT < 2 THEN RETURN
    GUI.RIGHT = GUI.LEFT + GUI.WIDTH - 1
    GUI.BOTTOM = GUI.TOP + GUI.HEIGHT - 1
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT + 1, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH - 2, 1, GUI.EDGE.H, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT + 1, GUI.BOTTOM, GUI.WIDTH - 2, 1, GUI.EDGE.H, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP + 1, 1, GUI.HEIGHT - 2, GUI.EDGE.V, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.RIGHT, GUI.TOP + 1, 1, GUI.HEIGHT - 2, GUI.EDGE.V, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, 1, 1, GUI.CORNER.TL, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.RIGHT, GUI.TOP, 1, 1, GUI.CORNER.TR, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT, GUI.BOTTOM, 1, 1, GUI.CORNER.BL, GUI.BORDER
    GP.FILL GUI.RIGHT, GUI.BOTTOM, 1, 1, GUI.CORNER.BR, GUI.BORDER
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.CLOSE -- give the pixels back, if we took a copy.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.CLOSE:
    IF GUI.STASHED <> 0 THEN GP.RESTR GUI.BANK
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.SIZE -- how big the box has to be.
## **
## **  Width is the widest thing that must fit; height is the message, a
## **  blank, the caller's rows, and the two frame rows. The 4 on the
## **  width is two frame columns and a column of air each side, because
## **  text against a frame is hard to read.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.SIZE:
    GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 0
    IF GUI.MSG$ <> "" THEN GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 1
    IF GUI.MSG2$ <> "" THEN GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 2
    GUI.INNER.WIDTH = GUI.BODY.WIDTH
    IF LEN(GUI.MSG$) > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.MSG$)
    IF LEN(GUI.MSG2$) > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.MSG2$)
    IF LEN(GUI.TITLE$) + 2 > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.TITLE$) + 2
    GUI.GAP.ROWS = 0
    IF GUI.HEAD.ROWS > 0 THEN GUI.GAP.ROWS = 1
    GUI.WIDTH = GUI.INNER.WIDTH + GUI.PADX * 2 + 2
    GUI.HEIGHT = GUI.HEAD.ROWS + GUI.GAP.ROWS + GUI.BODY.ROWS + GUI.PADY * 2 + 2
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.PLACE.BOX -- centred, or where the caller said.
## **
## **  Clamped at 0 on both axes so a box too big for the mode is merely
## **  cut off at the right and bottom, rather than drawn at a negative
## **  coordinate -- which VERA would happily write somewhere else.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.PLACE.BOX:
    GOSUB GUI.SCREEN
    IF GUI.PLACE <> 0 THEN GUI.LEFT = GUI.X : GUI.TOP = GUI.Y : GOTO GUI.PLACE.SCROLL
    GUI.LEFT = INT((GUI.SCREEN.COLS - GUI.WIDTH) / 2)
    GUI.TOP = INT((GUI.SCREEN.ROWS - GUI.HEIGHT) / 2)
    IF GUI.LEFT < 0 THEN GUI.LEFT = 0
    IF GUI.TOP < 0 THEN GUI.TOP = 0

## ---- LAST, and on BOTH paths. The offset turns a screen row into a map
## ---- row, and every coordinate from here on -- the frame, the text, and
## ---- the rectangle GP.STASH saves -- is a map row. Applying it to the
## ---- explicit path too means GUI.X and GUI.Y stay screen coordinates,
## ---- which is the only reading under which the two paths agree.
GUI.PLACE.SCROLL:
    GUI.TOP = GUI.TOP + GUI.ROW.OFFSET
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.SCREEN -- the mode's size, asked rather than assumed.
## **
## **  NOT CACHED. A program that switches mode between two dialogs would
## **  otherwise place the second one against the first one's screen. The
## **  call is one KERNAL entry on a path that is about to wait for a
## **  human, so there is nothing to save by remembering it.
## **
## **  The floors are for a mode that reports something unusable; 80x60 is
## **  the machine's own default and a safe thing to be wrong towards.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.SCREEN:
    GP.CALL GUI.SCRMODE, 0, 0, 0, 1
    GUI.SCREEN.COLS = GP.X : GUI.SCREEN.ROWS = GP.Y
    IF GUI.SCREEN.COLS < 20 THEN GUI.SCREEN.COLS = 80
    IF GUI.SCREEN.ROWS < 8 THEN GUI.SCREEN.ROWS = 60
    RETURN

GUI.MODULE.END:
#ENDIF
